1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,285
In the woods of northern Maine,

2
00:00:02,305 --> 00:00:03,998
during the Second World War,

3
00:00:04,018 --> 00:00:06,396
an American guard handed a German prisoner

4
00:00:06,416 --> 00:00:08,795
a shovel and told him to dig.

5
00:00:08,815 --> 00:00:10,870
The prisoner was fifteen years old.

6
00:00:12,075 --> 00:00:14,962
He started to dig, and then he started to cry.

7
00:00:14,982 --> 00:00:16,707
When the guard asked him why,

8
00:00:16,727 --> 00:00:19,033
the boy said he believed he was digging

9
00:00:19,053 --> 00:00:19,905
his own grave.

10
00:00:19,925 --> 00:00:22,521
He was sure that when the hole was deep

11
00:00:22,541 --> 00:00:23,995
enough, they would shoot him.

12
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:26,724
It was a latrine pit.

13
00:00:26,744 --> 00:00:29,196
The guards comforted him, and the boy went

14
00:00:29,216 --> 00:00:30,760
back to the camp alive.

15
00:00:32,075 --> 00:00:34,351
This is the documented story of the boys

16
00:00:34,371 --> 00:00:36,360
who were sent to America as German

17
00:00:36,380 --> 00:00:37,221
prisoners of war:

18
00:00:37,241 --> 00:00:39,230
what they had been told to expect,

19
00:00:39,250 --> 00:00:41,526
and what they found, told from their own

20
00:00:41,546 --> 00:00:43,555
words and the people who guarded them.

21
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:49,001
By 1944, Germany was running out of men.

22
00:00:49,021 --> 00:00:51,061
As the war turned against it,

23
00:00:51,081 --> 00:00:53,809
the army drafted younger boys and older men.

24
00:00:53,829 --> 00:00:55,869
At the camp in Houlton, Maine,

25
00:00:55,889 --> 00:00:58,273
the prisoners were fifteen years of age

26
00:00:58,293 --> 00:00:59,093
and older.

27
00:01:00,225 --> 00:01:02,448
One of them was Gerhard Kleindt.

28
00:01:02,468 --> 00:01:04,691
He had been drafted at seventeen.

29
00:01:04,711 --> 00:01:06,933
Before he was captured in Normandy,

30
00:01:06,953 --> 00:01:09,924
he had been told that the Americans did

31
00:01:09,944 --> 00:01:11,065
not take prisoners.

32
00:01:12,450 --> 00:01:14,818
One night his convoy was attacked,

33
00:01:14,838 --> 00:01:17,603
and his vehicle overturned in the gunfight.

34
00:01:17,623 --> 00:01:20,787
He was certain he would be shot immediately.

35
00:01:20,807 --> 00:01:23,573
Instead, American soldiers pulled him to safety.

36
00:01:23,593 --> 00:01:25,961
They took him in for questioning,

37
00:01:25,981 --> 00:01:28,746
and they fed him milk and cornflakes.

38
00:01:28,766 --> 00:01:30,736
The Americans, he said later,

39
00:01:30,756 --> 00:01:31,950
saved his life.

40
00:01:33,250 --> 00:01:34,898
Another boy was Hank Welzel.

41
00:01:34,918 --> 00:01:37,899
He was twelve when he was in the Hitler

42
00:01:37,919 --> 00:01:40,234
Youth, and sixteen when the German army

43
00:01:40,254 --> 00:01:40,901
drafted him.

44
00:01:40,920 --> 00:01:43,902
He was trained as a medic and sent to

45
00:01:43,922 --> 00:01:46,590
fight the Americans and the British in Italy.

46
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:49,224
Welzel had a secret.

47
00:01:49,244 --> 00:01:51,913
He had been born in the United States.

48
00:01:51,933 --> 00:01:54,602
His grandfather had always told him to be

49
00:01:54,622 --> 00:01:56,618
proud that he was an American,

50
00:01:56,638 --> 00:01:59,307
but he never dared to tell the other

51
00:01:59,327 --> 00:01:59,979
German soldiers.

52
00:01:59,999 --> 00:02:02,331
You had to consider yourself a German,

53
00:02:02,351 --> 00:02:05,040
he said, if you wanted to stay alive.

54
00:02:06,750 --> 00:02:09,610
On October the tenth, 1944,

55
00:02:09,630 --> 00:02:11,596
just short of his eighteenth birthday,

56
00:02:11,616 --> 00:02:14,575
Welzel was captured on a hill north of Florence.

57
00:02:14,595 --> 00:02:16,560
Now he faced a new question.

58
00:02:16,580 --> 00:02:19,208
Should he tell the Americans where he had

59
00:02:19,228 --> 00:02:20,028
been born?

60
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:22,440
He did not.

61
00:02:22,460 --> 00:02:24,960
He never told anybody, he said.

62
00:02:24,980 --> 00:02:27,080
It was his highest secret.

63
00:02:28,450 --> 00:02:30,442
He was shipped across the Atlantic to the

64
00:02:30,462 --> 00:02:31,951
country where he had been born,

65
00:02:31,971 --> 00:02:34,466
and he spent the rest of the war in a

66
00:02:34,486 --> 00:02:35,975
prisoner of war camp in Alabama,

67
00:02:35,995 --> 00:02:36,795
keeping that secret.

68
00:02:38,525 --> 00:02:40,579
Kleindt was sent to Maine.

69
00:02:40,599 --> 00:02:43,068
Camp Houlton sat in Aroostook County,

70
00:02:43,088 --> 00:02:43,897
potato country.

71
00:02:43,917 --> 00:02:46,801
About three thousand five hundred men were

72
00:02:46,821 --> 00:02:51,375
held there between 1944 and 1946.

73
00:02:52,075 --> 00:02:53,702
One of the American guards,

74
00:02:53,722 --> 00:02:55,678
Milton Bailey, remembered how they looked

75
00:02:55,698 --> 00:02:56,666
when they arrived.

76
00:02:56,686 --> 00:02:58,313
They were really just boys,

77
00:02:58,333 --> 00:03:00,289
he said, ragged, in torn clothing,

78
00:03:00,309 --> 00:03:02,615
with not much meat on their bones.

79
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:05,044
Maine needed them.

80
00:03:05,064 --> 00:03:07,883
So many local men were overseas that the

81
00:03:07,903 --> 00:03:11,076
farmers had no one to bring in the harvest.

82
00:03:11,096 --> 00:03:13,560
The prisoners picked potatoes and cut wood

83
00:03:13,580 --> 00:03:14,979
for the paper mills.

84
00:03:14,999 --> 00:03:16,753
One of them, Rudi Richter,

85
00:03:16,773 --> 00:03:18,882
remembered that they helped pick potatoes

86
00:03:18,902 --> 00:03:21,740
and got paid a few cents per barrel.

87
00:03:23,025 --> 00:03:26,184
They were paid in credit, about a dollar a

88
00:03:26,204 --> 00:03:29,009
day, to spend at the camp store on

89
00:03:29,029 --> 00:03:30,422
chocolate, tobacco and paint.

90
00:03:30,442 --> 00:03:32,894
The University of Maine offered them classes.

91
00:03:32,914 --> 00:03:34,660
Kleindt cooked for the guards,

92
00:03:34,680 --> 00:03:36,779
then worked on a potato farm,

93
00:03:36,799 --> 00:03:39,625
and took part in the camp's education program.

94
00:03:40,325 --> 00:03:42,888
If you want more prisoner of war stories

95
00:03:42,908 --> 00:03:45,605
told from the documents, subscribe to WWII

96
00:03:45,625 --> 00:03:46,425
Frontlines.

97
00:03:47,875 --> 00:03:50,525
The boys had been raised in a hard

98
00:03:50,545 --> 00:03:52,528
military world, where even small mistakes

99
00:03:52,548 --> 00:03:53,195
were punished.

100
00:03:53,215 --> 00:03:55,866
One day a young prisoner working in a

101
00:03:55,886 --> 00:03:57,868
farmer's kitchen knocked over a colander

102
00:03:57,888 --> 00:03:58,536
of apples.

103
00:03:58,556 --> 00:04:01,206
He broke down in tears and covered his

104
00:04:01,226 --> 00:04:02,895
head, waiting to be hit.

105
00:04:04,175 --> 00:04:05,179
Nobody hit him.

106
00:04:05,199 --> 00:04:07,568
The farm family could not speak German,

107
00:04:07,588 --> 00:04:09,956
and he could not speak much English,

108
00:04:09,976 --> 00:04:10,980
but he understood.

109
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:13,369
He went back to the barracks smiling,

110
00:04:13,389 --> 00:04:15,095
carrying a box of apples.

111
00:04:16,450 --> 00:04:18,876
At Christmas, a farmer named Fenton Shaw

112
00:04:18,896 --> 00:04:21,322
decided that the prisoners on his farm

113
00:04:21,342 --> 00:04:24,118
should celebrate the way they did at home.

114
00:04:24,138 --> 00:04:26,214
His family brought Christmas stockings for

115
00:04:26,234 --> 00:04:27,962
every prisoner, filled with homemade

116
00:04:27,982 --> 00:04:29,030
treats and fruit.

117
00:04:30,300 --> 00:04:31,984
That night, the prisoners gathered outside

118
00:04:32,004 --> 00:04:33,972
their barracks in the winter night and

119
00:04:33,992 --> 00:04:35,392
sang Stille Nacht, Silent Night,

120
00:04:35,412 --> 00:04:37,400
to the people who had brought them.

121
00:04:38,625 --> 00:04:42,148
Not everyone in Houlton was happy to have them.

122
00:04:42,168 --> 00:04:44,904
Some of the prisoners were dedicated Nazis.

123
00:04:44,924 --> 00:04:48,053
But a museum curator in Maine would later

124
00:04:48,073 --> 00:04:51,597
say that a lot of them were reluctant soldiers.

125
00:04:51,617 --> 00:04:53,585
Many were young and frightened.

126
00:04:55,350 --> 00:04:58,170
When the war ended, the boys did not go

127
00:04:58,190 --> 00:04:58,801
straight home.

128
00:04:58,821 --> 00:05:01,030
They went back to a ruined country.

129
00:05:02,300 --> 00:05:04,831
Rudi Richter could not recognise his own hometown.

130
00:05:04,851 --> 00:05:07,701
So much of it had been destroyed that he

131
00:05:07,721 --> 00:05:08,339
was lost.

132
00:05:08,359 --> 00:05:11,209
He stopped an old man in the street and

133
00:05:11,229 --> 00:05:13,780
asked him the way to his family's house.

134
00:05:15,050 --> 00:05:17,407
Sixty years later, telling the story,

135
00:05:17,427 --> 00:05:18,200
Richter wept.

136
00:05:18,220 --> 00:05:21,369
The old man had not been a stranger.

137
00:05:21,389 --> 00:05:23,370
It was his own father.

138
00:05:24,675 --> 00:05:27,760
Hank Welzel worked in France after the war.

139
00:05:27,780 --> 00:05:30,866
Then he went to the American embassy in

140
00:05:30,886 --> 00:05:32,807
Paris and told his secret.

141
00:05:32,827 --> 00:05:36,115
In 1949, at twenty-three, he arrived in

142
00:05:36,135 --> 00:05:38,411
the United States with nothing but his

143
00:05:38,431 --> 00:05:41,055
German accent and a suitcase of dirty laundry.

144
00:05:42,400 --> 00:05:44,767
A year later, the United States Army

145
00:05:44,787 --> 00:05:45,449
drafted him.

146
00:05:45,469 --> 00:05:48,176
He went to Korea as an American medic,

147
00:05:48,196 --> 00:05:51,586
and he came home with a Purple Heart and a

148
00:05:51,606 --> 00:05:52,268
Bronze Star.

149
00:05:52,288 --> 00:05:54,314
He had fought in two wars,

150
00:05:54,334 --> 00:05:55,337
under two flags.

151
00:05:55,357 --> 00:05:57,383
He had nightmares for forty-five years,

152
00:05:57,403 --> 00:06:00,110
and he and his wife kept his German

153
00:06:00,130 --> 00:06:02,497
service a secret until their children were

154
00:06:02,517 --> 00:06:03,540
in high school.

155
00:06:05,250 --> 00:06:08,478
In 2003, the town of Houlton invited its

156
00:06:08,498 --> 00:06:09,697
old prisoners back.

157
00:06:09,717 --> 00:06:12,133
Four of them came from Germany,

158
00:06:12,153 --> 00:06:13,351
now old men.

159
00:06:13,371 --> 00:06:15,788
Veterans gave them a motorcycle escort,

160
00:06:15,808 --> 00:06:18,650
and the town made them honorary citizens.

161
00:06:20,050 --> 00:06:21,968
Not everyone was comfortable with it.

162
00:06:21,988 --> 00:06:24,551
But one of the four said the experience

163
00:06:24,571 --> 00:06:27,458
had changed his life, and the way he saw

164
00:06:27,478 --> 00:06:28,770
people and judged them.

165
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:31,648
Rudi Richter did something more.

166
00:06:31,668 --> 00:06:33,983
During the war, a potato farmer's wife

167
00:06:34,003 --> 00:06:35,984
named Catherine Bell had treated her

168
00:06:36,004 --> 00:06:37,985
prisoners with dignity, though her own

169
00:06:38,005 --> 00:06:40,320
brother had been lost in the war,

170
00:06:40,340 --> 00:06:41,655
his body never recovered.

171
00:06:41,675 --> 00:06:43,989
Sixty years later, Richter went with her

172
00:06:44,009 --> 00:06:46,324
to the edge of the Baltic Sea,

173
00:06:46,344 --> 00:06:48,993
the final resting place of the brother she

174
00:06:49,013 --> 00:06:49,813
had lost.

175
00:06:50,975 --> 00:06:54,007
Gerhard Kleindt looked back on it all in a

176
00:06:54,027 --> 00:06:54,685
single sentence.

177
00:06:54,705 --> 00:06:56,720
The best years of my life,

178
00:06:56,740 --> 00:06:59,433
he said, were spent in a prison camp

179
00:06:59,453 --> 00:07:00,451
behind barbed wire.

180
00:07:00,471 --> 00:07:03,164
It was the consequence of a senseless war

181
00:07:03,184 --> 00:07:05,538
that Germany had started, for which we

182
00:07:05,558 --> 00:07:06,575
were shamelessly misused.

183
00:07:08,250 --> 00:07:10,418
If your family farmed in Aroostook County,

184
00:07:10,438 --> 00:07:12,918
or if your father or grandfather guarded a

185
00:07:12,938 --> 00:07:15,418
prisoner of war camp in Maine or anywhere

186
00:07:15,438 --> 00:07:18,250
in America, tell us about it in the comments.

187
00:07:20,025 --> 00:07:22,797
The prisoners at Houlton left something behind.

188
00:07:22,817 --> 00:07:25,190
The camp had an art program,

189
00:07:25,210 --> 00:07:26,785
and the men painted.

190
00:07:26,805 --> 00:07:29,577
One unknown prisoner painted a portrait of

191
00:07:29,597 --> 00:07:31,172
a local couple's daughter,

192
00:07:31,192 --> 00:07:33,166
an eight-year-old girl named Lois,

193
00:07:33,186 --> 00:07:33,986
smiling.

194
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:37,440
Their paintings still hang in Houlton today.

195
00:07:37,460 --> 00:07:40,251
They were painted by boys who had once

196
00:07:40,271 --> 00:07:42,380
believed that America would shoot them.

